(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-electrocardiography-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Electrocardiography Quiz 2 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why are the augmented leads considered unipolar?","choices":["They measure between two physical electrodes","Only one physical electrode is used","They are placed on the chest rather than the limbs","They record no voltage difference at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The machine constructs the other half of the comparison, so there is only one real point of view involved. The rest being created by the machine. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where is the U wave best seen, and what is it thought to represent?","choices":["In lead II, representing atrial repolarization","In V2 and V3, possibly repolarization of the Purkinje fibers","In aVR, representing septal depolarization","In V5 and V6, representing delayed ventricular filling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the hedging is honest: there is no clear understanding of what it represents, so the association with electrolytes is more useful than the theory. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why is lead II used for rhythm interpretation?","choices":["It has a near-perfect view along the heart's vector","It is the only lead that records atrial activity","It is unaffected by patient movement","It is the only bipolar lead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The conduction pathway runs left and down, and lead II looks along that line, so the complexes are at their clearest. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where does the sinoatrial node sit?","choices":["At the junction of the atria and the ventricles","Within the interventricular septum","In the upper wall of the right atrium","In the wall of the left atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and being the highest pacemaker in the chain is what makes it the one that normally sets the rate. Just below the opening of the vena cava. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are contiguous leads?","choices":["Leads placed physically next to one another on the skin","Leads that share a single physical electrode","Two or more leads looking at the same area of the heart","Leads recorded at the same moment in time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why a change appearing in a contiguous group means more than the same change in one isolated lead. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does depolarization represent, and what is its relationship to contraction?","choices":["Recovery of cardiac tissue, and it follows contraction","Activation of cardiac tissue, occurring simultaneously with contraction","The resting state of the cell between beats","Activation of cardiac tissue, and it precedes contraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the order matters: the electrical event comes first, so an electrocardiogram showing depolarization does not prove the muscle actually contracted. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which property refers to a cardiac cell's ability to produce its own impulse?","choices":["Automaticity","Excitability","Conductivity","Contractility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is why the heart beats without instruction from anywhere else, and why several parts of it can take over pacemaking if the one above fails. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does a BIPOLAR lead measure?","choices":["The voltage difference between two distinct body points","The electrical activity from one electrode reference point","The average of all ten electrodes simultaneously","The mechanical movement of the chamber beneath it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two real electrodes with a defined polarity, which is what separates leads I, II and III from the rest. One positive and one negative. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is the TP segment the best place to assess the isoelectric line?","choices":["It is the longest segment on the tracing","It represents the resting state of the cardiac cells, between one T wave and the next P wave","It contains the J point","It is unaffected by lead placement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nothing electrical is happening there, so it gives the truest baseline against which to judge elevation or depression elsewhere. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which of these can an electrocardiogram NOT tell you?","choices":["Whether there is electrical activity in the heart","Where the impulse originates","Whether there are conduction problems","Whether the patient has a pulse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with hemodynamic status and cardiac output. It shows electrical activity, and electrical activity without a pulse is a recognized and lethal state. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Into how many fascicles does the left bundle branch divide, and what are they?","choices":["Two: the left anterior and the left posterior","Four, one to each wall of the ventricle","Three: left anterior, left posterior and septal","It does not divide; it terminates directly in Purkinje fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The septal fascicle is the one most often forgotten, which matters because the three-fascicle arrangement is what makes bifascicular block possible. The left anterior. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which property allows an impulse to spread from one myocyte to the next?","choices":["Conductivity","Automaticity","Excitability","Contractility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is what turns a single cell's discharge into a coordinated wave across the whole muscle. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does a lead record when the overall vector is net zero?","choices":["A negative deflection","A positive deflection","Electrical noise","An isoelectric line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A flat trace is a finding rather than an absence of one: it says the forces canceled in that lead's direction. With no deflection. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the ionic arrangement during phase 4, the resting state?","choices":["More potassium inside the cell","More sodium inside the cell, with potassium outside","Equal concentrations of all ions across the membrane","Calcium inside the cell with potassium outside"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That separation is the stored charge the cell spends when it fires, and restoring it is what the later phases achieve. With sodium and calcium outside. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A standard twelve-lead electrocardiogram uses how many physical electrodes?","choices":["Ten","Twelve","Six","Fifteen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each electrode is a different point of view, and the machine derives twelve views from the ten it is given. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In what order do the five phases of the cardiac action potential occur?","choices":["Phase 0, then 1, 2, 3 and 4","Phase 4, then 0, 1, 2 and 3","Phase 1, then 2, 3, 4 and 0","Phase 4, then 3, 2, 1 and 0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The numbering does not run in order, which is exactly why it is worth learning as a sequence rather than as a list: rest is phase four, and the upstroke that follows it is phase zero. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What happens to the membrane during depolarization?","choices":["It becomes more negative","It becomes less negative","It holds steady at its resting voltage","It loses all charge separation entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cell moves away from its resting negativity, which is what the word depolarization describes. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which internodal tract does the majority of the population use?","choices":["The middle tract","The inferior posterior, or slow, tract","All three equally","The superior anterior, or fast, tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The other two usually terminate before reaching the atrioventricular node, so the fast tract does most of the work. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How much time does one small box on electrocardiogram paper represent?","choices":["0.2 seconds","0.1 seconds","0.04 seconds","1 second"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and five of them make a large box at 0.2 seconds, so five large boxes cover one second. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What must a deflection do to count as a true S wave?","choices":["Extend below the baseline","Follow the T wave","Exceed one small box in amplitude","Appear in every lead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If it does not, it is called an S wave pattern instead, which is a distinction of naming that matters when describing a tracing precisely. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which leads are bipolar?","choices":["aVR, aVL and aVF","V1 through V6","Leads I, II and III","All twelve leads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They form the imaginary triangle across the shoulders and the left lower body, and everything else on a twelve-lead tracing is unipolar. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is noted about the length of the right bundle branch?","choices":["It is much shorter than the left, in proportion to the smaller right ventricle","It is much longer than the left, despite the left ventricle being the larger chamber","The two are of equal length","It does not reach the Purkinje network"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the counterintuitiveness is the point: the anatomy does not follow chamber size. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the peak of the T wave mark?","choices":["The end of ventricular depolarization","The start of atrial repolarization","The beginning of the ST segment","The dividing line between the absolute and the relative refractory period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes a stimulus landing near the T wave peak so consequential: the cell has just become excitable again. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A corrected QT of 520 milliseconds is reported. How should that be read?","choices":["Normal","Borderline prolongation","Shortened","High risk prolongation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beyond five hundred milliseconds is the high risk band, above the borderline range that begins in the low four hundreds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the J point?","choices":["The peak of the T wave","The start of the P wave","The junction where the QRS complex ends and the ST segment begins","The end of the T wave"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it should sit at the baseline, with about a millimeter of variance tolerated. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the PR interval include?","choices":["Atrial depolarization, the delay at the atrioventricular node, and conduction through the His-Purkinje system","Ventricular depolarization and repolarization","Atrial depolarization only","The resting state of the cardiac cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three events in one measurement, which is why a prolonged PR interval does not by itself say where the hold-up is. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the QT interval span?","choices":["From the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex","From the start of the QRS complex to the end of the T wave","From the end of the T wave to the next P wave","Between two consecutive R waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which makes it all of the ventricular activity in one cardiac cycle, depolarization and repolarization together. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What characterizes phase 1, early repolarization?","choices":["Calcium channels open, producing contraction","Sodium channels close and potassium channels reopen","Fast sodium channels open","The membrane returns fully to minus ninety millivolts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The inward current stops and an outward one resumes, which begins bringing the membrane back down. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does a cardiac vector represent?","choices":["The direction of blood flow through the chambers","The strength of contraction alone","The direction and strength of the electrical impulse","The position of the heart within the chest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both quantities at once, which is why it is drawn as an arrow rather than described as a line. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is the corrected QT used rather than the raw QT interval?","choices":["The raw QT cannot be measured accurately on standard paper","The corrected value uses different units","The raw QT applies only to men","The QT interval changes with heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Without correcting, the same heart would appear to have a different QT at different rates. The correction accounts for it. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
